  "description": "Erection of a single, freestanding bike store in front garden",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "<p>The development hereby permitted must be begun not later than the expiration of three years beginning with the date of this permission.</p><p><br></p><p>\r\n</p><p>\r\nREASON: In order to comply with the provisions of the Town and Country Planning Act 1990 (as amended).</p><p>\r\n</p>",
    "<p>The Development hereby permitted shall only be carried out and completed strictly in accordance with the submitted plans hereby approved and any subsequent approval of details.\r\n</p><p><br></p><p>\r\nREASON: To ensure that the development hereby permitted is carried out in full accordance with the plans hereby approved.\r\n</p>",
    "&lt;div style=&quot;color: rgb(34, 34, 34); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: small;&quot;&gt;In order to avoid soil compaction, the floor under the bike shed will be installed using a &#x27;no-dig&#x27; method and consist of a 75mm deep cellular confinement panel filled with a clean, open graded angular non-calcareous aggregate (20-40mm or 4mm - 20mm). Cellular confinement system (CCS) structures are 3D, honeycomb-like geocells used in Tree Root Protection Areas (RPAs) to create stable, free-draining surfaces without compacting the soil or damaging roots, fulfilling BS5837:2012 guidance by using clean, angular stone infill to maintain water and air flow to the trees. These systems act as a flexible, load-distributing raft, reducing vertical stress on the subgrade and preventing root damage.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;color: rgb(34, 34, 34); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: small;&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;im&quot; style=&quot;color: rgb(80, 0, 80); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: small;&quot;&gt;REASON: To protect retained tree(s) pursuant to BS5837(2012): Trees in relation to design, demolition and construction, in line with the requirements of Section 197(a) of the Town and Country Planning Act 1990&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;"
  ],
